History of Operating Systems (Simple Beginner Version)

Operating systems (OS) changed slowly over many years.
As computers improved, operating systems also improved.

To understand OS history, we study different generations of computers because each generation had different technology and different types of operating systems.

The changes did not happen suddenly.
Old and new technologies existed together for some time.

Charles Babbage and the First Computer Idea

The first real idea of a digital computer was given by Charles Babbage.

He designed a machine called the Analytical Engine.

But he could not fully build it because machines at that time were not accurate enough.

This machine had:

gears
wheels
mechanical parts

It did not have an operating system.

Ada Lovelace

Ada Lovelace worked with Babbage.

She is called the first programmer in history.

The programming language Ada was named after her.

First Generation (1945–1955)
Vacuum Tube Computers

Early computers used vacuum tubes.

These computers were:

very large
very slow
very expensive
very weak compared to modern computers

Some famous early computers were:

ENIAC
Colossus
Mark I
Alan Turing also worked on early computers.

These computers could take seconds just to solve simple calculations.

Programming in First Generation

There were:

no operating systems
no programming languages
no assembly language

Programmers directly worked with machine instructions.

Sometimes they even connected wires manually using plugboards.

The same people:

designed the computer
built it
programmed it
operated it
repaired it

Everything was done manually.

How Computers Were Used

A programmer:

booked computer time
went to the computer room
connected cables or plugboards
ran the program

Programs were mostly used for:

mathematical calculations
artillery calculations
tables of sine and cosine values
Punched Cards

Later, punched cards were introduced.

Instead of manually wiring cables, programs were written on cards.

The computer read instructions from these cards.

This made work a little easier.

Second Generation (1955–1965)
Transistors and Batch Systems

In the second generation, transistors replaced vacuum tubes.

This made computers:

smaller
faster
more reliable

Now computers could run properly for longer periods.

Because of this, different people got different jobs:

designers
programmers
operators
maintenance workers
Mainframe Computers

Large computers called mainframes were used.

Only:

big companies
universities
government offices

could afford them.

These computers were kept inside special air-conditioned rooms.

How Programming Worked

The programmer:

wrote the program on paper
converted it into punched cards
gave the cards to the operator
waited for the output

The operator:

loaded the cards into the computer
ran the program
printed the output

This wasted a lot of time because operators had to move around constantly.

Batch System (Very Important)

To reduce wasted time, the batch system was introduced.

Idea of Batch System

Instead of running one job at a time:

many jobs were collected together
all jobs were stored on magnetic tape
the computer processed them one after another automatically

This group of jobs was called a batch.

Simple Flow of Batch Processing
Programmers submit punched cards
Cards are copied to magnetic tape
Main computer reads jobs from tape
Computer runs jobs automatically
Output is stored on another tape
Output is later printed

This reduced computer idle time.

Early Operating System

A simple program was used to:

load jobs
run jobs
automatically start the next job

This program became the early form of an operating system.

Control Cards

Special cards were used to control jobs.

Examples:

$JOB → information about the job
$FORTRAN → load FORTRAN compiler
$LOAD → load compiled program
$RUN → run the program
$END → end of job

These control cards later became the idea behind:

shells
command line interpreters
